# Cursor Rules Location

How to add new cursor rules to the project

1. Always place rule files in PROJECT_ROOT/.cursor/rules/:

    ```
    .cursor/rules/
    ├── your-rule-name.mdc
    ├── another-rule.mdc
    └── ...
    ```

2. Follow the naming convention:

    - Use kebab-case for filenames
    - Always use .mdc extension
    - Make names descriptive of the rule's purpose

3. Directory structure:

    ```
    PROJECT_ROOT/
    ├── .cursor/
    │   └── rules/
    │       ├── your-rule-name.mdc
    │       └── ...
    ├── AMV-ConnEx/
    │   ├── Controllers/
    │   ├── Models/
    │   ├── Views/
    │   └── ...
    ├── AMV-ConnEx.xcodeproj/
    ├── AMV-ConnEx.xcworkspace/
    └── ...
    ```

4. Never place rule files:

    - In the project root
    - In subdirectories outside .cursor/rules
    - In any other location

5. **IMPORTANT: Never add .cursor/ to Xcode project**

    - Never drag `.cursor/` folder or any files from it into Xcode
    - Never use "Add Files to..." in Xcode for any files in `.cursor/`
    - These are configuration files for Cursor, not code files for your app
    - If accidentally added, remove the file reference from Xcode (right-click → Delete → Remove Reference)

6. Cursor rules have the following structure:

```
---
description: Short description of the rule's purpose
globs: optional/path/pattern/**/*.swift
alwaysApply: false
---

# Rule Title

Main content explaining the rule with markdown formatting.

1. Step-by-step instructions
2. Code examples
3. Guidelines

Example:

```swift
// Good example
class GoodExample {
    private var property: String
    
    func goodMethod() {
        // Implementation following guidelines
    }
}

// Bad example
class BadExample {
    var property: String  // Should be private
    
    func badMethod() {
        // Implementation not following guidelines
    }
}
```
```
